"""Class 10 SST Ch 4 The Age of Industrialisation - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Merchants in Europe began to move to the countryside to get goods produced for the world market.", r"Town craft guilds were powerful and restricted the entry of new merchants into the trade.", "a",
     r"Guilds controlled prices and production in towns, so merchants turned to peasants and cottagers in villages (proto-industrialisation)."),
 AR4(r"The spinning jenny was invented by James Hargreaves in 1764.", r"Richard Arkwright created the cotton mill.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but they are separate inventions and R does not explain A."),
 AR4(r"In Victorian Britain the upper classes preferred handmade goods.", r"Machine-made goods were considered finer and a symbol of class.", "c",
     r"A is true. Handmade goods were a mark of refinement and class; machine-made ones were for the masses, so R is false."),
 AR4(r"Indian weavers' textile exports to Britain increased in the nineteenth century.", r"Cheap machine-made cotton goods from Manchester flooded the Indian market.", "d",
     r"Exports of Indian cloth declined as British manufactures replaced them, so A is false; R is true."),
 AR4(r"Industrialists employed jobbers to recruit workers for their factories.", r"Jobbers were usually old, trusted workers who got people from their villages and helped them settle in the city.", "a",
     r"Jobbers were the link between workers and the factory owner; R explains why they were employed."),
 AR4(r"The first cotton mill in Bombay came up in 1854.", r"The first jute mill in Bengal was set up in 1855.", "b",
     r"Both true, being facts from the early factory age in India; neither explains the other."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> When the spinning jenny was introduced in the woollen industry, women who survived on hand spinning began attacking the new machines. The jenny speeded up the spinning process and reduced the labour demand. Yet in Britain, hand labour continued to be widely used in many industries. Industrialists did not always prefer machines as they were costly and repairs were expensive. "
   r"(i) Who invented the spinning jenny? (ii) Why did women attack the machine? (iii) Give one reason why many industrialists preferred human labour. (iv) Why was handmade production still preferred in some goods?",
   r"(i) James Hargreaves (1). (ii) They feared unemployment, as the jenny reduced the demand for hand labour (1). (iii) Labour was plentiful and cheap; machines needed big capital and repairs (1). (iv) Demand was seasonal and varied in design, and the upper classes valued the finish of handmade goods (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> After the East India Company established political power, it tried to eliminate competition from traders and brokers and gain more direct control over weavers. It appointed paid servants called gomasthas to supervise weavers, collect supplies and examine the quality of cloth. Weavers were given advances and could not sell to other buyers. "
   r"(i) Who were gomasthas? (ii) What was the purpose of appointing them? (iii) What was given to weavers to bind them to the Company? (iv) How did the system affect weavers?",
   r"(i) Paid servants of the Company who supervised weavers (1). (ii) To eliminate traders and brokers and control weaving directly (1). (iii) Loans or advances (1). (iv) Weavers lost bargaining power, were punished for delays and could not sell to others, so many fell into debt and left weaving (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> When Manchester industrialists began selling cloth in India, they put labels on the cloth bundles so that buyers could see the place of manufacture and the name of the company. Many labels carried images of Indian gods and goddesses. Calendars were also printed to popularise products among people who could not read. Indian manufacturers' advertisements often gave a nationalist message. "
   r"(i) What was the purpose of labels? (ii) Why were images of Indian gods used? (iii) Why were calendars used for advertising? (iv) What nationalist message did Indian manufacturers give?",
   r"(i) To show the place of manufacture and the company name, a sign of quality (1). (ii) To give the goods a divine approval and make them familiar to Indian buyers (1). (iii) They could be understood even by people who could not read and were displayed all year (1). (iv) If you care for the nation, buy products that Indians produce (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

